# Test: remove-private-as replace-with peer-as applies to ORIGINATED routes
# VALIDATES: RFC 6996 Section 4 on the session write gate, in `replace-with peer-as`
# VALIDATES: mode: the substituted ASN is the EXPORT DESTINATION peer AS (65002),
# VALIDATES: not the local AS (64500).
# PREVENTS: exportFilterForBody passing the wrong destPeerAS to the export chain.
# PREVENTS: the STRIP-only sibling test passing while peer-as substitution is unwired
# PREVENTS: (STRIP cannot distinguish a wrong peerAS -- it never uses it).
#
# The remove-vs-replace choice is vendor policy: RFC 6996 mandates removal from the
# AS path (Section 4) but does not specify a substitution, so `replace-with peer-as`
# is a Ze design decision, not an RFC requirement. What RFC 6996 Section 4 does
# require, and what this test guards, is that 64512 does not reach the EBGP peer.
# See plan/spec-fixit-private-asn-leak.md.

stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=tcp_connections:value=1
option=timeout:value=20s
cmd=api:conn=1:seq=1:text=peer * update text origin igp as-path [64496 64512 64497] nhop 1.1.1.1 nlri ipv4/unicast add 10.0.0.0/24
# Private 64512 replaced by peer AS 65002 (0xFDEA), and the local AS 64500 (0xFBF4)
# prepended ahead of the operator's path per RFC 4271 S5.1.2:
# AS_PATH=AS_SEQUENCE[64500 64496 65002 64497].
# attrs(0x20=32) = ORIGIN(4) + AS_PATH(21) + NEXT_HOP(7). 0xFDEA is the receiver's
# remote AS; a local-AS substitution would encode the local AS here and fail this
# assertion, which is what this test exists to catch.
#
# The local AS is 64500, NOT a private ASN, and that matters. It used to be 65000,
# which is inside RFC 6996's private range 64512-65534, so once the local AS is
# prepended the export filter below replaces ZE'S OWN AS with the peer's -- yielding
# AS_PATH[65002 ...] toward the peer whose AS is 65002, which that peer must drop as
# a loop. Peering externally from a private ASN is the RFC 6996 problem this test was
# never trying to exercise; using a non-private local AS keeps the subject (the
# substituted ASN is the PEER AS, not the local AS) and drops the accident.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF003B02000000204001010040021202040000FBF40000FBF00000FDEA0000FBF140030401010101180A0000
EOF_PEER


stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
plugin {
	external remove-private-as-replace-originated {
		run "ze-test fixture plugin/remove-private-as-replace-originated"
		encoder json
	}
}
bgp {
	policy {
		remove-private-as REPLACE {
			replace-with peer-as
		}
	}
	peer receiver-peer {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 64500
				remote 65002
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}
		filter {
			export [ remove-private-as:REPLACE ]
		}
		attach process remove-private-as-replace-originated {
			send update
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze-peer --port $PORT:stdin=peer
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze --plugin ze.bgp-filter-remove-private-as -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=15s
reject=stderr:pattern=panic
reject=stderr:pattern=fatal error
